OEM shells stage combined stdout/stderr but explicitly report child status.
Both successful child status and exact byte count were required. There was
no error text masquerading as binary data. Raw ADB exec lacks shell-v2 status,
so it was not used for the actual physical reads.

## Offline interpretation (bounded inspection, not full reverse engineering)

Capstone and ARM binutils agree on checked little-endian ARM code. Internal
Thumb execution was not substantiated; BX alone is not evidence of Thumb.

Reset vector0x0 branches0x20; seven other vectors branch0x18c..0x1ec.
Startup switches privileged modes, preserves arguments in banked FIQ
registers and performs clock-region RMWs. Literal addresses include
902ba4,902ba0,902b80,90214c,902280, inside the live clock-controller resource.

One early selector is:
  000000ac ldr r0,[pc,#0x290] ; literal344 contains00706034
  000000b0 ldr r1,[r0]
  000000b4 tst r1,#1
  000000b8 bne c0
  000000bc mov pc,#20000
Bit0 clear reaches the unconditional PC assignment; bit0 set continues ROM
startup. This is NOT identified here as a secure-boot-enable flag, nor was the
register read live. Destination20000 agrees with the inspected RPM image's
load address, but its control-register semantics/path selection need analysis.

Continuing startup sets SP=2e000 and reaches bx r0 at188, target8ed0.
The8ed0 path has another selector using helper8890 and bits100/80 at20002c;
one path dispatches through43fe0, another initializes state then enters6720.
Boot main walks a function-pointer table at10ce0 and hands off via bx r1 at67bc.
These are static control-flow observations, not traces of this boot.

Initialization templates and writable destinations are separate:
  table11f3c: copy124c0 ->43fe0, length20
  table11f48: copy12050 ->20358, length64
  table11f5c: clear20008, length1e0
  table11fa4: copy120b4 ->206a8, length94
  table11f90: copy122f8 ->26a4c, length198
Copy helper268 reads r1 and writes r0; clear helper2bc writes zeros. Exception
stubs use pointers43fe4..43ffc, matching the copied default table. This layout
strongly fits fixed ROM with separate SRAM work areas, rather than a random
RAM acquisition. An identical RAM mirror/ROM alias remains observationally
possible from these reads alone.

